[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Time Series Collections in MongoDB"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Time series collections in MongoDB are optimized for storing sequences of measurements or events over time. They provide efficient storage and retrieval of time-based data, making them suitable for applications such as IoT sensor data, financial data, and monitoring systems."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a Time Series Collection"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a time series collection, you use the 'createCollection' command with the 'timeseries' option. You must specify the 'timeField', which indicates the field containing the timestamp for each document. Optionally, you can specify a 'metaField' to store metadata that is common to multiple measurements within a single time series, and a 'granularity' to optimize storage based on the expected data density."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"db.createCollection("sensorData", {
  timeseries: {
    timeField: "timestamp",
    metaField: "sensorId",
    granularity: "seconds"
  }
})"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'sensorData' is the name of the collection. 'timestamp' is the field containing the timestamp, 'sensorId' is the field containing the sensor identifier (metadata), and 'granularity' is set to 'seconds', indicating that data points are expected to be recorded at the second level."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Inserting Data into a Time Series Collection"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inserting data into a time series collection is similar to inserting data into a regular collection. Each document must contain the 'timeField' and any other relevant data fields. If a 'metaField' is defined, you can include it in the first document of a time series. Subsequent documents in the same time series can omit the 'metaField' to save space."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"db.sensorData.insertMany([
  {
    sensorId: "sensor1",
    timestamp: ISODate("2023-10-27T10:00:00Z"),
    temperature: 25.5
  },
  {
    timestamp: ISODate("2023-10-27T10:00:01Z"),
    temperature: 25.6
  },
  {
    sensorId: "sensor2",
    timestamp: ISODate("2023-10-27T10:00:00Z"),
    temperature: 22.1
  }
])"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we insert three documents into the 'sensorData' collection. The first document for 'sensor1' includes the 'sensorId' (metaField). The second document for 'sensor1' omits the 'sensorId' because it's already associated with that time series. The third document represents data from 'sensor2' and includes its 'sensorId'."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Querying Time Series Data"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Querying time series data is similar to querying regular collections. You can use the standard MongoDB query operators to filter data based on the 'timeField', 'metaField', or any other data fields. MongoDB automatically optimizes queries on time series collections for improved performance."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// Find all data points for sensor1 within a specific time range
db.sensorData.find({
  sensorId: "sensor1",
  timestamp: {
    $gte: ISODate("2023-10-27T10:00:00Z"),
    $lt: ISODate("2023-10-27T10:00:05Z")
  }
})"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"This query retrieves all data points for 'sensor1' where the 'timestamp' is greater than or equal to '2023-10-27T10:00:00Z' and less than '2023-10-27T10:00:05Z'."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Considerations"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The 'timeField' must be indexed for optimal query performance."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Choose the appropriate 'granularity' based on the expected data density. Smaller granularities (e.g., 'seconds') are suitable for high-frequency data, while larger granularities (e.g., 'hours' or 'days') are better for lower-frequency data."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consider using the 'metaField' to store metadata that is common to multiple measurements within a single time series. This can reduce storage space and improve query performance."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Time series collections are immutable; you cannot update documents after they are inserted. If you need to update data, you must insert a new document with the updated values."},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Time series collections provide a powerful and efficient way to store and query time-based data in MongoDB. By understanding the concepts and considerations outlined in this tutorial, you can effectively leverage time series collections for your applications."}]
